<body>
<header class="topbar">
  <div class="logo" aria-hidden="true"><span></span></div>
  <h1>Universal Metering Programme — Financial and Delivery Model</h1>
  <nav class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Sheets">
    <button class="tab" role="tab" data-view="dashboard" aria-selected="true">Overview</button>
    <button class="tab" role="tab" data-view="strategy" aria-selected="false">Strategy</button>
    <button class="tab" role="tab" data-view="bmc" aria-selected="false">Business model</button>
    <button class="tab" role="tab" data-view="contracts" aria-selected="false">Contracts and payments</button>
    <button class="tab" role="tab" data-view="data" aria-selected="false">Data sheet</button>
    <button class="tab" role="tab" data-view="whos-who" aria-selected="false">Stakeholders</button>
    <button class="tab" role="tab" data-view="risk" aria-selected="false">Sensitivity and risk</button>
    <button class="tab" role="tab" data-view="future" aria-selected="false">Future initiatives</button>
    <button class="tab" role="tab" data-view="help" aria-selected="false">User guide</button>
  </nav>
</header>
<div class="toolbar">
  <button class="tbtn drawer-btn" id="drawerBtn" aria-controls="side" aria-expanded="false">Assumptions</button>
  <label>Scenario <select class="sel" id="scenSel"></select></label>
  <label>Currency <select class="sel" id="curSel"><option value="usd">USD</option><option value="ngn">₦ Naira</option></select></label>
  <label>View <select class="sel" id="viewSel"><option value="meter">One meter</option><option value="programme">Whole programme</option></select></label>
  <span class="spacer"></span>
  <button class="tbtn primary" id="copyBtn">Copy data (CSV)</button>
  <button class="tbtn" id="resetBtn">Reset</button>
</div>

<div class="shell">
  <aside class="side" id="side" aria-label="Key levers and cards">
    <h2>Find</h2>
    <div class="search"><input id="cardSearch" type="search" placeholder="Search sections" aria-label="Search sections"></div>
    <select class="sel" id="figSel" aria-label="Go to a key figure" style="width:100%;margin:0 0 8px;font-weight:400"></select>
    <div id="cardList"></div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:14px">Principal assumptions</h2>
    <p class="hint">The eight assumptions with the greatest effect on results, shown in the selected reporting currency. All other inputs are maintained in the Data sheet.</p>
    <div id="levers"></div>
    <label class="toggle"><input type="checkbox" id="pctToggle"> Fee as % of each top-up (off = flat fee)</label>
  </aside>

  <main>
    <noscript><div class="flag warn">This model calculates in the browser. Open the live link with JavaScript on.</div></noscript>

    <!-- DASHBOARD -->
    <section class="view on" id="v-dashboard" aria-label="Dashboard">
      <div class="canvas">
        <article class="card span2 a-all" data-card="answer" data-group="Answer" data-title="Summary of findings">
          <header><h3>Summary of findings</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="verdict" id="verdict"></p><div class="kpis" id="k-answer"></div><div id="answerFlag"></div></div>
        </article>
        <article class="card a-all" data-card="whopays" data-group="Answer" data-title="Funding of meter capital">
          <header><h3>Funding of meter capital</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="lead" id="whopaysLead"></p><svg class="chart" id="ch-whopays" viewBox="0 0 400 120" role="img" aria-label="Who pays"></svg><div class="legend" id="lg-whopays"></div>
          <div id="upkeepGap"></div></div>
        </article>

        <article class="card span3 a-all" data-card="brief" data-group="Answer" data-title="Response to the brief">
          <header><h3>Response to the brief</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body">
            <p class="verdict">Proposed approach: a cost-sharing framework, delivered as meter-as-a-service, with results-based utility payments.</p>
            <p class="lead">Households pay a small capped metering charge with each energy purchase; the utility pays an explicit contribution tied to verified gains; government provides bounded first-loss funding from money already earmarked. Meters are supplied, installed and maintained as a service, and providers are paid per functioning meter.</p>
            <div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>Starter question</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead><tbody>
              <tr><td>1. Who should pay for a meter, and why is that fair?</td><td>Those who benefit, in proportion: households a small capped charge (they get a verifiable bill and maintained service); the utility a contribution from verified cash gains and loss reduction (it gains most); government bounded first loss (a public good, using earmarked funds). Nobody pays twice: the charge replaces the existing metering surcharge and earlier buyers are credited.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2. How can a meter be paid for without a large upfront charge?</td><td>Capital grants and, later, affordable debt pay for meters upfront through a protected account at an existing licensed bank. Households repay part as a percentage of each token; providers are paid only on verified commissioning.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>3. Where should the first million meters go?</td><td>Meters already delivered but not installed, then ready feeders in low-metering, high-loss areas, with a fairness floor for every region. Within each feeder: low-income unmetered households, people owed refunds, and faulty or obsolete meters first. See Geographic rollout.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>4. How do you protect the model from an import duty or currency drop?</td><td>Contract clauses: price split and indexed with a supplier risk band, batch prices fixed at call-off, change-in-law clause placing duty changes with the funding authority, local kit assembly, milestone payments and consignment stock. The household charge stays capped. See Contracts &amp; payments.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>5. How do you keep free-meter and paid-meter customers from feeling treated unfairly?</td><td>One rule for everyone from day one: the same capped metering charge for upkeep, whoever paid for the meter. Earlier buyers’ outstanding refunds become automatic credits on every token. New meters are allocated by need and readiness, not tariff band.</td></tr>
            </tbody></table></div>
            <h4 style="margin:12px 0 4px">Success metrics and evidence (live)</h4>
            <div class="tw"><table class="txt" id="t-metrics"></table></div>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="card a-all" data-card="start" data-group="Plan" data-title="Starting point">
          <header><h3>Starting point</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="lead">The brief’s scenario. Nigeria is the reference case.</p><div class="kpis" id="k-start"></div>
          <details class="more"><summary>Nigeria reference (NERC, Aug 2026)</summary>
            <div class="line"><span>Metered</span><b>63.10% (8.05M of 12.75M)</b></div>
            <div class="line"><span>Unmetered</span><b>4.70M</b></div>
            <div class="line"><span>Installed in August</span><b>141,746</b></div>
            <div class="line"><span>Net pace after new customers</span><b>~0.8M a year</b></div>
            <div class="line"><span>Installed in 2024 (target 800,000)</span><b>547,436</b></div>
          </details></div>
        </article>
        <article class="card span2 a-reg" data-card="discos" data-group="Plan" data-title="Rollout priorities by DisCo">
          <header><h3>Rollout priorities by DisCo (Nigeria reference)</h3><span class="owner a-reg">Regulator data</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="lead">Bars show unmetered customers (where the volume is); colour shows 2025 ATC&amp;C losses (where the utility gain from metering is largest). Source: NERC; editable on the Data sheet.</p>
          <svg class="chart" id="ch-discos" viewBox="0 0 520 300" role="img" aria-label="Unmetered customers by DisCo, coloured by losses"></svg>
          <div class="legend"><span><i style="background:var(--bad)"></i>Losses 55% or more</span><span><i style="background:var(--warn)"></i>40–55%</span><span><i style="background:var(--bar)"></i>Below 40%</span><span><i style="background:var(--line)"></i>No data</span></div>
          <div class="flag warn" id="discoReading"></div>
          <details class="more"><summary>Table (click a column heading to sort)</summary><div class="tw"><table id="t-disco"></table></div></details>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-top:8px">Context: metering applies to customers already connected. Access to electricity is a separate gap: 61% of Nigerians had access in 2023, about 33% in rural areas and 80–89% in urban areas (World Bank). Rural households without a connection belong to the solar and mini-grid branch.</p></div>
        </article>
        <article class="card a-local" data-card="rollout" data-group="Plan" data-title="Rollout and installer capacity">
          <header><h3>Rollout and installer capacity</h3><span class="owner a-local">Local installers</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="lead">Bars: installs per year by type against installer capacity (left axis). Line: metered share and number of metered customers at year end (right axis).</p>
          <svg class="chart" id="ch-rollout" viewBox="0 0 400 210" role="img" aria-label="Installs per year by type, installer capacity and metered share"></svg>
          <div class="legend"><span><i style="background:var(--bar)"></i>New meters</span><span><i style="background:var(--warn)"></i>Replacements (failed or destroyed)</span><span><i style="background:var(--muted)"></i>Legacy meters (DisCo)</span><span><i style="background:transparent;border-top:2px dashed var(--bad);height:0;width:14px"></i>Installer capacity</span><span><i style="background:var(--good);height:3px;width:16px"></i>Metered share · customers metered</span></div>
          <div class="chips" style="margin-top:8px;align-items:center"><span class="lead" style="margin:0">Change the inputs in the Data sheet:</span>
            <button class="tbtn" onclick="gotoGroup('Rollout')">Rollout</button><button class="tbtn" onclick="gotoGroup('Network and premises')">Network and premises</button><button class="tbtn" onclick="gotoGroup('Wear and damage')">Wear and damage</button></div>
          <div id="capFlag"></div>
          <details class="more"><summary>Year-by-year table</summary><div class="tw"><table id="t-rollout"></table></div>
          <ul class="plain"><li>Two queues ranked together: dense urban clusters (more installs a day) and high-loss feeders (bigger DisCo gain).</li></ul></details></div>
        </article>
        <article class="card span2 a-op" data-card="geo" data-group="Plan" data-title="Geographic rollout and the first million">
          <header><h3>Geographic rollout and the first million meters</h3><span class="owner a-op">Metering Programme Operator</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="lead">Areas are ranked on need (unmetered share, estimated billing), readiness (green and amber feeders) and utility losses, with a fairness floor: every region receives at least half its proportional share of meters each year. Nigerian DisCo areas are used as the reference; security and access conditions adjust the order locally.</p>
          <div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Where (Nigeria reference)</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>Year 1</td><td>Enugu State pilots; delivered stock installed in every DisCo area; first waves in Ibadan, Benin and ready feeders in Kano and Kaduna</td><td>Proof of service; Ibadan has the largest absolute gap (about 1.2M unmetered); fairness floor for the north</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Year 2</td><td>Enugu DisCo area incl. Anambra (Onitsha), Ibadan, Benin, Port Harcourt; north ramps up as readiness surveys finish</td><td>Proven regional packages expand</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Year 3</td><td>Jos, Yola, Kaduna, Kano at full speed; Abuja remainder</td><td>Lowest metering (about 31–36%), high losses</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Years 4–5</td><td>Lagos remainder (Ikeja, Eko already about 86%); red feeders after upgrades; rural solar and mini-grid cohorts</td><td>Complete coverage; long tail prepared from year 1</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <h4 style="margin:10px 0 4px">The first million meters</h4>
          <div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Approx. meters</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>Delivered but not installed (DISREP reference, Aug 2026)</td><td>~0.35M</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Existing provider pipelines converted to no-upfront installs</td><td>~0.2M (estimate)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ready feeders in low-metering, high-loss areas</td><td>~0.45M (estimate)</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-top:6px">Within each feeder: low-income unmetered households, people owed refunds and faulty or obsolete meters first, then street by street. In the model, the first million is reached within year 1.</p></div>
        </article>
        <article class="card a-disco" data-card="network" data-group="Plan" data-title="Network and premises readiness">
          <header><h3>Network and premises readiness</h3><span class="owner a-disco">DisCo</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="lead">A meter only helps on a network that can carry the load. The bar shows today’s unmetered customers by feeder readiness (assumptions until the feeder survey is done).</p>
          <svg class="chart" id="ch-ready" viewBox="0 0 400 56" role="img" aria-label="Unmetered customers by feeder readiness"></svg>
          <div id="readyCap" style="margin:6px 0 4px"></div>
          <div class="chips" style="margin:4px 0 8px;align-items:center"><span class="lead" style="margin:0">Change the shares in the Data sheet:</span><button class="tbtn" onclick="gotoGroup('Network and premises')">Network and premises</button></div><div class="kpis" id="k-network"></div><div id="netFlag"></div>
          <details class="more"><summary>Readiness rules</summary><div class="tw"><table class="txt">
            <thead><tr><th>Feeder</th><th>Work needed</th><th>Who pays</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>Green</td><td>Nothing beyond the meter</td><td>Meter programme</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Amber</td><td>Service cable, meter box, rewiring</td><td>Meter programme (in unit cost)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Red</td><td>Transformer or line upgrade first</td><td>DisCo or DFI capital, not the meter fee</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Legacy meters</td><td>Replace obsolete meters</td><td>DisCo, free to customer (NERC Order 246/2021)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details></div>
        </article>
        <article class="card a-map" data-card="cost" data-group="Plan" data-title="Cost of one meter">
          <header><h3>Cost of one installed meter</h3><span class="owner a-map">Meter providers</span></header>
          <div class="body"><svg class="chart" id="ch-cost" viewBox="0 0 400 190" role="img" aria-label="Meter cost build-up"></svg>
          <details class="more"><summary>Cost lines and procurement levers</summary><div id="costLines"></div>
          <ul class="plain"><li>Pooled tender across DisCos.</li><li>Local assembly from kits: lower duty and currency exposure.</li><li>Supplier absorbs part of FX and duty shocks (risk band).</li><li>Regional lots open to local SMEs.</li></ul></details></div>
        </article>
        <article class="card a-map" data-card="tech" data-group="Plan" data-title="Technology: standard or smart">
          <header><h3>Technology: standard or smart (IoT)</h3><span class="owner a-map">Meter providers</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="lead">Smart where it pays back, standard where it does not.</p><div class="kpis" id="k-tech"></div>
          <details class="more"><summary>Proposed mix</summary><ul class="plain">
            <li>Smart meters on distribution transformers: show where energy is lost.</li>
            <li>Smart household meters on high-loss feeders and for high-spend customers.</li>
            <li>Standard prepaid meters for low-spend households, with an upgrade path.</li>
            <li>Reuse the meter data platform DISREP is procuring.</li></ul></details></div>
        </article>

        <article class="card a-bank" data-card="flow" data-group="Money" data-title="Money flow and payment order">
          <header><h3>Money flow and payment order</h3><span class="owner a-bank">Licensed bank</span></header>
          <div class="body">
            <div class="flowrow">
              <div class="node a-customer"><b>Customer</b>Buys a token</div><span class="arrow">→</span>
              <div class="node a-disco"><b>DisCo token server</b>Splits off the fee</div><span class="arrow">→</span>
              <div class="node a-bank"><b>Licensed bank</b>Pays in fixed order</div>
            </div>
            <ol class="order"><li>Upkeep: repair, wear and damage reserve</li><li>Local bonds (senior)</li><li>DFI loan</li><li>Public first loss (last; absorbs the shortfall)</li></ol>
            <div class="kpis" id="k-flow"></div>
            <details class="more"><summary>Fee stacking</summary><p class="lead">For pilot customers the meter fee replaces the existing metering surcharge in the tariff, so it is one charge, not three. Open question: the surcharge funds free meters today; what replaces it?</p></details></div>
        </article>
        <article class="card a-disco" data-card="collections" data-group="Money" data-title="Collections and DisCo cash gain">
          <header><h3>Collections and DisCo cash gain</h3><span class="owner a-disco">DisCo</span></header>
