    <!-- STRATEGY -->
    <section class="view" id="v-strategy" aria-label="Strategy">
      <p class="lead" style="margin:0 0 12px;color:var(--muted)">B2B strategy for utilities and mini-grid operators. Mandatory Brief 15 scenario, USD reporting and basic end-customer safeguards. The updated planner uses a yearly FX path. Contracts and the payment flow are on the Contracts &amp; payments tab.</p>
      <div class="canvas">
        <article class="card span3 a-all" data-card="st-problem"><header><h3>Problem and design challenge</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="verdict">Provide households with functioning, maintained meters through one trusted service interface, without upfront meter payments, using transparent charges, an explicit utility contribution and clearly bounded public support.</p>
          <p><b>Problem.</b> Millions of households lack functioning electricity meters and face bills they cannot verify. Upfront costs, fragmented delivery, unclear maintenance responsibilities and conflicting incentives make getting and keeping a meter difficult. This erodes trust, weakens payments and limits funding for further rollout.</p>
          <p><b>Design challenge.</b> How might we give every household access to functioning, maintained metering within five years without large upfront payments or unaffordable ongoing charges, while keeping utilities financially viable and public funding requirements transparent?</p>
          <p class="lead">This programme is for metering and accountable service. It does not promise improvements such as cheaper electricity, uninterrupted supply or installation cost recovery that the evidence does not support.</p></div></article>

        <article class="card span3 a-all" data-card="st-goals"><header><h3>Five-year goals</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Target by year 5</th><th>Measured by</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>Coverage</td><td><b>100% of households with a functioning meter</b> (assignment target). Planning scenario 90–95%; any residual gap reported explicitly.</td><td>Functioning meters, not installations announced</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Trust</td><td>Households understand the offer and their receipts; metering and billing complaints down 60%; no unpaid refund promises</td><td>Commissioning checks, complaints, credits paid</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Transparency</td><td>Expenditure, receipts, complaints and maintenance published quarterly per area</td><td>Quarterly report, reconciled accounts</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Accountability</td><td>90% of appointments kept; repairs within the tiered limits; remedies applied when a party fails</td><td>Service records, independent verification</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Affordability</td><td>No upfront meter payment; metering charge a small, capped share of the bill; no duplicate charging. Ability to pay for electricity itself depends on tariffs and subsidies, which are outside this model and are addressed in the B2C exercise and tariff policy.</td><td>Actual household cash burden, in naira</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Money</td><td>Full cost per functioning meter and ongoing service cost known by segment; shortfall secured before scaling; public support bounded and visible</td><td>Reconciled ledgers</td></tr>
          </tbody></table></div>
          <div class="flag warn">Disclaimer: 100% in five years is ambitious. It assumes funding released on time, every red feeder upgraded, no procurement delays and installs of about 1.8–2.1M a year from year 1. The 90–95% scenario is a planning range requiring validation.</div></div></article>

        <article class="card span3 a-all" data-card="st-roadmap"><header><h3>Roadmap: faster from year 1</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><div class="gyears"><span>Year 1</span><span>Year 2</span><span>Year 3</span><span>Year 4</span><span>Year 5</span></div>
          <div class="gantt">
            <div class="gbar a-reg" style="grid-column:1/13;grid-row:1">Year 1: establish trust, accelerate</div>
            <div class="gbar a-op" style="grid-column:13/25;grid-row:2">Year 2: stabilise</div>
            <div class="gbar a-bank" style="grid-column:25/37;grid-row:3">Year 3: scale responsibly</div>
            <div class="gbar a-local" style="grid-column:37/61;grid-row:4">Years 4–5: complete and sustain</div>
          </div>
          <div class="gcov"><div>~56%</div><div>~69%</div><div>~81%</div><div>~92%</div><div>100% target</div></div>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-top:6px">Metered share at year end (model, base case: about 1.8M installs in year 1, then 2.0–2.1M). Trust-building and acceleration run in parallel, not in sequence.</p>
          <h4 style="margin:10px 0 4px">How year 1 speeds up instead of slowing down</h4>
          <ul class="plain"><li>Install meters already delivered but not installed (Nigeria reference: about 365,000 DISREP meters delivered but not installed in Aug 2026) under the new payment-on-commissioning rule.</li>
          <li>Convert existing provider pipelines to no-upfront installs immediately, instead of waiting for new procurement.</li>
          <li>Run contrasting pilots in parallel, not one after another.</li>
          <li>Award regional delivery packages through framework call-offs so installers start within weeks.</li>
          <li>Readiness surveyed per feeder, so ready feeders are installed street by street.</li></ul></div></article>

        <article class="card a-reg phase" data-card="st-p1"><header><h3>Year 1 – Establish trust and accelerate</h3><span class="owner a-reg">Regulator-led</span></header>
          <div class="body"><h4>Priorities</h4><ul class="plain"><li>Confirm household segments; mandate the Metering Programme Operator; set up the agreements.</li><li>Test assisted channels, receipts, installation explanations and maintenance in contrasting pilots.</li><li>Install delivered stock and convert existing pipelines (see roadmap).</li><li>Scrap: upfront payments, DisCo-dependent refunds, cash at the door, surcharge stacking, money before readiness, duplicate reports.</li><li>Defer: customer credit products, wider IoT, complex financing.</li></ul>
          <h4>Expansion condition</h4><ul class="plain"><li>Customers understand the offer; payments reconcile; service works; next-batch funding is committed.</li></ul></div></article>

        <article class="card a-op phase" data-card="st-p2"><header><h3>Year 2 – Stabilise</h3><span class="owner a-op">Operator-led</span></header>
          <div class="body"><h4>Priorities</h4><ul class="plain"><li>Improve appointments, repairs and back-end records.</li><li>Expand proven regional packages.</li><li>Test limited operational innovation (back-end AI for feeder ranking and fair estimates).</li></ul>
          <h4>Expansion condition</h4><ul class="plain"><li>Delivery and customer outcomes remain satisfactory as volume increases.</li></ul></div></article>

        <article class="card a-bank phase" data-card="st-p3"><header><h3>Year 3 – Scale responsibly</h3><span class="owner a-bank">Licensed bank</span></header>
          <div class="body"><h4>Priorities</h4><ul class="plain"><li>Replicate proven service.</li><li>Validate utility benefits (results-based contribution).</li><li>Introduce debt only where affordable cash supports repayment.</li></ul>
          <h4>Expansion condition</h4><ul class="plain"><li>Full service cost and funding are demonstrated by segment.</li></ul></div></article>

        <article class="card a-local phase" data-card="st-p4"><header><h3>Years 4–5 – Complete and sustain</h3><span class="owner a-local">Local delivery</span></header>
          <div class="body"><h4>Priorities</h4><ul class="plain"><li>Deliver rural and complex cohorts prepared from year 1.</li><li>Maintain installed assets.</li><li>Address the residual gap and report it explicitly.</li></ul>
          <h4>Expansion condition</h4><ul class="plain"><li>Expansion does not undermine existing service or leave unfunded obligations.</li></ul></div></article>

        <article class="card span3 a-op" data-card="st-argument"><header><h3>The argument for the set-up</h3><span class="owner a-op">Metering Programme Operator</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p><b>Metering Programme Operator:</b> a clearly mandated delivery function within an existing organisation, not a new institution. Government retains funding authority; an existing licensed bank executes agreed payment rules, with independent reconciliation. Statutory powers, procurement accountability and independent regulation are preserved.</p>
          <div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>Delivery gap it fills</th><th>Duplicated task it removes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>No one resolves delivery failures across contractors</td><td>Each DisCo, programme and provider chasing its own handoffs</td></tr>
            <tr><td>No proof that meters work after installation</td><td>Separate installation reporting to NERC, fund managers and DisCos</td></tr>
            <tr><td>No single view of expenditure, complaints and maintenance</td><td>Parallel monthly returns in different formats</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Late warning of procurement delays and funding gaps</td><td>Programme-by-programme procurement tracking</td></tr>
            <tr><td>No way to replace a failing provider without abandoning customers</td><td>Customer-by-customer escalation to the regulator</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <h4 style="margin:10px 0 4px">What it offers government</h4><ul class="plain"><li>One party responsible for resolving delivery failures across contractors.</li><li>Verified functioning meters, rather than installation announcements alone.</li><li>Clear visibility of expenditure, customer complaints and maintenance.</li><li>Earlier warning of procurement delays and funding shortfalls.</li><li>A way to stop or replace underperforming providers without abandoning customers.</li></ul>
          <h4 style="margin:10px 0 4px">Conditions</h4><ul class="plain"><li>Full cost disclosed, with the work and costs it replaces; net benefit demonstrated. If an existing organisation can deliver the same through a clearer mandate, a separate operator is unnecessary.</li><li>Affordability, existing entitlements and prevention of duplicate charging are explicit pilot conditions.</li><li>Delegated operational decisions defined precisely.</li><li>Bounded pilot, capped expenditure, termination rights and an orderly handover plan.</li></ul>
          <p class="lead">The strongest argument: the arrangement helps government, regulators and utilities deliver their existing commitments with fewer unresolved handoffs and clearer evidence of results.</p></div></article>

        <article class="card span2 a-customer" data-card="st-customers"><header><h3>Customer segments</h3><span class="owner a-customer">Customer</span></header>
          <div class="body"><div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Main problem</th><th>Required response</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>Unmetered, low-income households</td><td>Upfront cost, unpredictable bills, limited ability to absorb increases</td><td>No upfront payment, assisted registration, affordability assessment, funded protections</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Other unmetered households</td><td>Unclear waiting lists, estimated bills, poor communication</td><td>Published eligibility, appointment windows, transparent payment terms</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tenants and shared properties</td><td>Unclear authority to install, shared connections, inherited account disputes</td><td>Landlord–tenant process, clear account responsibility, technical assessment</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Faulty or obsolete meters</td><td>Loss of service, replacement delays, disputed balances</td><td>Repair and replacement pathway, balance reconciliation, clear responsibility</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rural and hard-to-reach households</td><td>Higher delivery costs, weak channels, network constraints</td><td>Geographic service packages, community access points, separately funded extra costs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-top:6px">Small businesses are a later, separately assessed segment and are not used to disguise the economics of serving low-income households. Prioritisation combines need, readiness and fair geographic coverage, never spending or ease of installation alone.</p></div></article>

        <article class="card a-customer" data-card="st-value"><header><h3>Value proposition: the household promise</h3><span class="owner a-customer">Customer</span></header>
          <div class="body"><ul class="plain"><li>No upfront programme meter payment.</li><li>A clear explanation of what electricity and metering charges cover.</li><li>A functioning meter, verified at commissioning.</li><li>One contact for appointments, faults and complaints.</li><li>A visible repair process and accountable maintenance provider.</li><li>Protection against duplicate meter-cost recovery.</li><li>Access without a smartphone or a credit score.</li></ul>
          <h4 style="margin:10px 0 4px">To test in pilots</h4><ul class="plain"><li>Ability to pay: actual household payments against income, supply and seasons (the model’s bill is an estimate).</li><li>How often households engage: daily top-ups or occasional.</li><li>Whether a subscription-style option fits some households.</li><li>How simple sign-up can be: one USSD step, an agent visit, or a click.</li></ul></div></article>

        <article class="card a-agent" data-card="st-channels"><header><h3>Channels: one service, several ways in</h3><span class="owner a-agent">Agents &amp; fintechs</span></header>
          <div class="body"><div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Primary channels</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>Understand the offer</td><td>Community demonstrations, utility communications, local-language radio and SMS, trusted community organisations</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Register, check eligibility</td><td>Assisted desk, telephone, authorised agents, simple digital option</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Appointments, progress</td><td>SMS or telephone, with a physical alternative</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Buy energy, get a receipt</td><td>Existing authorised vending and payment channels</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Faults and complaints</td><td>One programme contact and case reference; independent escalation kept</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <ul class="plain"><li>App first covers status, receipts, appointments and fault reporting only.</li><li>Agents carry visible ID; unofficial fees prohibited; agents paid for verified, valid outcomes, not registrations collected.</li></ul></div></article>

        <article class="card a-op" data-card="st-relationship"><header><h3>Customer relationship</h3><span class="owner a-op">Metering Programme Operator</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p>A <b>Meter Service Coordinator</b> within the Metering Programme Operator is accountable from first contact through maintenance and eventual replacement.</p>
          <p class="lead">Understand → register once → assess → appointment → install and explain → confirm operation → support and repair.</p>
          <h4 style="margin:8px 0 4px">At commissioning the household learns</h4><ul class="plain"><li>How to read and use the meter.</li><li>How charges appear on receipts.</li><li>What happens to an existing balance or dispute.</li><li>How to report a fault.</li><li>Who pays for maintenance and replacement.</li></ul>
          <p class="lead">Trust is measured through understanding, resolved problems and functioning service. Reminders support appointments and receipts; no pressure messaging, punitive credit scoring or rewards that push households to buy energy they cannot afford.</p></div></article>

        <article class="card a-inv" data-card="st-funding"><header><h3>Revenue and funding</h3><span class="owner a-inv">Investors &amp; DFIs</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p>The household payment and the operator’s income are kept separate. The full cash-stream table and payment order are on the <b>Contracts &amp; payments</b> tab.</p>
          <div class="flag bad">Unresolved: the model does not show household charges covering ongoing costs. Before scaling, the strategy must secure the shortfall, reduce validated costs or demonstrate an affordable utility contribution.</div></div></article>

        <article class="card a-map" data-card="st-capability"><header><h3>Activities and resources</h3><span class="owner a-map">Delivery</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="lead">Core activities: onboarding, readiness coordination, procurement, installation scheduling, acceptance, payment reconciliation, maintenance management, complaints resolution.</p><ul class="plain"><li>Trained customer-service staff and qualified installers.</li><li>A reliable customer and asset register, with data protection.</li><li>Replacement stock, tools and regional service arrangements.</li><li>Protected accounts and competent reconciliation.</li><li>Standard contracts, procurement capability, independent verification.</li><li>Accessible communications and community relationships.</li><li>Working capital for delivery and maintenance.</li><li>Interoperable systems, portable data, contractual handover; no dependence on one supplier’s ecosystem.</li></ul></div></article>

        <article class="card a-all" data-card="st-partners"><header><h3>Partners: one interface, competition kept</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><ul class="plain"><li>One accountable Metering Programme Operator.</li><li>Participating utilities under common core conditions.</li><li>Regional delivery packages combining supply, installation and maintenance.</li><li>An existing licensed bank with a defined account-control mandate (no new bank).</li><li>Government funding and policy counterparts with distinct responsibilities.</li><li>Independent regulators, safety bodies and verification.</li><li>Community organisations help explain and test the service; they are not an approval layer.</li></ul><p class="lead">Standard contracts with local schedules. Common conditions do not mean pooling legally restricted funds or removing independent oversight.</p></div></article>

        <article class="card span2 a-op" data-card="st-costs"><header><h3>Cost structure, including B2C costs <span style="color:var(--bad)">(PLACEHOLDER – NOT PART OF THIS MODEL)</span></h3><span class="owner a-op">Metering Programme Operator</span></header>
          <div class="body"><div class="flag warn" style="color:var(--bad);font-style:italic">B2C is a separate next exercise. Listed here as a placeholder; not priced in this build.</div>
          <div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>Cost group</th><th>Items</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>Programme establishment</td><td>Contracting, system configuration, baseline research, staff mobilisation</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Customer acquisition and onboarding</td><td>Outreach, assisted registration, appointments, household explanations</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Installation</td><td>Hardware, freight, duty and tax, premises work, labour, testing, acceptance (in the model)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Customer relationship</td><td>Service desk, communications, complaints, accessibility, dispute administration</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Continuing operation</td><td>Maintenance, communications, replacement stock, reserves, system support (maintenance in the model)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Financial and governance</td><td>Banking, reconciliation, verification, audit, working capital, currency protection</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Equity and difficult locations</td><td>Costed protections, additional travel, rural logistics, readiness work</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-top:6px">Economies of scale through standard specifications, framework procurement, route planning and shared systems. Rural unit costs are not assumed to fall with national volume.</p></div></article>

        <article class="card a-map" data-card="st-service"><header><h3>Service levels for repair</h3><span class="owner a-map">Meter providers</span></header>
          <div class="body"><div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Limit</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>Urban</td><td>48 hours</td><td>90% year 1, 95% from year 3</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Peri-urban, towns</td><td>72 hours</td><td>90% from year 2</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Rural</td><td>5 working days</td><td>80% year 3, 90% year 5</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Remote, solar, mini-grid</td><td>7–10 days</td><td>Operator standard</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <ul class="plain"><li>From the moment a fault is reported, the household pays no metering charge and receives no estimated bill. The charge is collected only with token purchases, so it stops automatically while the meter cannot accept tokens.</li><li>Once the limit for the area is missed, a daily credit is added to the next token; the credit is the same everywhere.</li><li>Swap first, repair later.</li><li>Tiered limits need the regulator’s approval (NERC rule is 48 hours nationally).</li></ul></div></article>

        <article class="card span2 a-all" data-card="st-simplify"><header><h3>Simplification: parties grouped, today’s checkpoints removed</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>Today</th><th>After (per this model)</th><th>Overseen by</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>PMI, DISREP, MAF, NMMP with different rules</td><td>Common conditions; funding ledgers kept separate where the law requires</td><td>Government funding authority</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Providers chosen per DisCo</td><td>Regional delivery packages under one rule</td><td>Metering Programme Operator</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ad hoc installers, cash at the door</td><td>Certified local installers paid on verified outcomes</td><td>Delivery packages, verified</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Vending with no common rule</td><td>One split rule at the DisCo token server</td><td>Licensed bank, reconciled</td></tr>
            <tr><td>DisCo owns meters, refunds, customers</td><td>DisCo keeps network, token server and an explicit contribution</td><td>Lead regulator</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-top:6px">Today’s checkpoints removed: apply-and-wait at DisCo, clearance per customer, provider choice per order, payment verification, approval per batch, DisCo commissioning, refund approvals, duplicate reports. Kept: NEMSA certification, reconciliation, audit, data consent.</p></div></article>

        <article class="card a-gov" data-card="st-gov"><header><h3>Government engagement</h3><span class="owner a-gov">Government</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p>No programme to influence government. The case is made through this proposal and the normal approval processes.</p><ul class="plain"><li>Predictable import treatment for meters and kits.</li><li>Lawful household charge arrangements, reconciled with existing surcharges.</li><li>Defined funding commitments per batch.</li><li>Removal of duplicate administrative steps.</li></ul><p class="lead">Each request is tied to evidence from the pilots and to the government’s own commitments.</p></div></article>

        <article class="card a-all" data-card="st-change"><header><h3>Monitoring, reporting and change</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><p class="lead">Monthly performance review; concise quarterly public report covering:</p><ul class="plain"><li>Functioning coverage and customers waiting, by segment and geography.</li><li>Installation time, failed appointments, repair performance.</li><li>Household understanding, complaints, actual cash burden.</li><li>Full cost per functioning meter and ongoing service cost.</li><li>Reconciled receipts, utility contributions, subsidies, remaining commitments.</li><li>Procurement performance, conflicts of interest, material changes.</li></ul>
          <p class="lead">Innovation enters through bounded trials with a named owner, budget, success measure and stopping rule. Back-end AI may come early; credit products, wider IoT and complex financing need separate evidence.</p></div></article>

        <article class="card span2 a-all" data-card="st-realism"><header><h3>Realism check</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><div class="tw"><table class="txt"><thead><tr><th>Ambition</th><th>How it is handled</th></tr></thead><tbody>
            <tr><td>100% functioning meters in five years</td><td>Kept as the target with a disclaimer; 90–95% planning scenario; gap reported explicitly</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Faster rollout from year 1</td><td>Delivered stock, converted pipelines, parallel pilots, framework call-offs; depends on funding release</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Household charges covering costs</td><td>Not shown by the model; shortfall secured, costs cut or utility contribution proven before scaling</td></tr>
            <tr><td>48-hour repairs everywhere</td><td>Tiered limits with the same daily credit</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Stable import duty</td><td>Not assumed; contracts protect the programme (see Contracts &amp; payments)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Network upgrades</td><td>Outside the metering charge; sequenced through DisCo or DFI capital</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></article>

        <article class="card a-all" data-card="st-open"><header><h3>Open decisions and questions</h3><span class="owner a-all">All parties</span></header>
          <div class="body"><ul class="plain"><li>Next decisions: pilot setting, lawful household charge treatment, funding envelope.</li><li>Host organisation for the Metering Programme Operator (MAF fund manager, PMI delivery company or other).</li><li>Is the existing metering service charge already held in a protected account?</li><li>Import duty in force in 2026.</li><li>Funding estimate: use the updated five-year B2B planner. Original lifetime and meter-only figures remain in reference material and should not be mixed.</li><li>“X depreciation”: share of meter depreciation covered by charges, or tolerance for naira depreciation?</li></ul></div></article>
      </div>
